## Reviewer checklist (literal pass/fail)

For each `format_*` function the function-writer touched:

- [ ] No `.get()` calls (or each `.get()` has an explanatory comment about the observed missing field)
- [ ] No `trackstar_tags` assignment
- [ ] `additional_fields` is `{}` unless customer-specified
- [ ] No `IntegrationNotImplemented` used as fallback for present-but-null values
- [ ] No `x = data.get(y, IntegrationNotImplemented)` patterns
- [ ] No `x = y or IntegrationNotImplemented` patterns
- [ ] **Write-accepted-but-read-absent fields use `None`, not `IntegrationNotImplemented`.** If the vendor accepts a field on `create_*` / `update_*` (whether or not the integration implements writes yet) but doesn't echo it on the corresponding read, the read path sets `None` with a comment naming the asymmetry — not `IntegrationNotImplemented`. The vendor contract determines the absence value, not the integration's current method coverage. Check candidates: `unit_price`, `discount_amount`, `currency`, `note`, `b2b`, write-only metadata fields. Exception: a canonical `List`/`Dict` field whose schema rejects `None` (its `not_implemented_default` is `[]`/`{}`, so `allow_none` never auto-enables) takes `IntegrationNotImplemented` on the read side even when a write accepts it — with the asymmetry recorded in `api-summary.md`. → `[BLOCK]` when violated.
- [ ] **No `[]` as an absence value on structural-decomposition fields when the parent has data.** Use the vendor's per-element breakdown, or mirror the parent collection to the **single** child (canonical: `list(per_package_lines.values()) or shipment_line_items` — see shipbob). → `[BLOCK]` for an empty single-package `line_items` while the parent has data, and for an invented allocation. Exception: a vendor exposing **multiple** packages with no per-package breakdown correctly leaves every package's `line_items` empty (a fabricated split double-counts against the cross-package quantity validation), with the gap recorded in `api-summary.md`. Carve-out: event-stream collections like `inbound.receipts` are NOT structural decompositions; `receipts = []` is the correct starting state until items are received (see Inbound receipts decision tree).
- [ ] `created_date` / `updated_date` shape correct: both pulled when present; **missing one set equal to the present one** when vendor exposes only one (don't omit `created_date` while keeping `updated_date` — the pipeline backfilling `EPOCH` on the omitted side mislabels historical records as 1970); both omitted only when neither is sourceable. Never synthesized from `now()` or hardcoded dates on a read path (a `create_*` write originating the record may stamp `now()` as the true origin timestamp when the vendor echoes none — see the dates row above). → `[BLOCK]` when `created_date` is omitted while `updated_date` is set, or when either is synthesized on a read path. (The pipeline auto-copies `created_date` to a missing `updated_date`, so the integration may legitimately set only `created_date`; the inverse — only `updated_date` — is the failure case.)
- [ ] String/datetime fields don't have `or None` suffix
- [ ] No list/dict comprehensions in transformation logic
- [ ] No `logger.info` / `print` calls in integration code except carrier (carrier may use them at major async checkpoints; `logger.warning` / `logger.error` for genuinely surprising states is fine in any type)
- [ ] No docstrings
- [ ] Comments are lowercase, ~5-10 words, explain WHY
- [ ] **Comments pass the one-read test — plain English, no noun-stacks / arrow-chains / framework vocabulary.** Clarity wins over brevity; don't flag a clear comment just for running a little long.
- [ ] Comments ≤2 lines (one preferred). Multi-paragraph or 5+ line blocks only for genuinely complex situations.
- [ ] **No observation dates, customer orgs, or source-integration attribution in code comments.** Present-tense WHY only; rich rationale lives in `api-summary.md` "Field-mapping decisions" — not in the code.
- [ ] **Comments are self-contained — no references to `api-summary.md` or other gitignored files.** Inline the relevant context.
- [ ] **No doctrine vocabulary or authoring-agent attribution in code comments.** No `# tier-3 pattern`, `# per the warehouse decision tree`, `# canonical exemplar`, `# Claude/AI suggested`, `# config-driven`, etc. Comments describe local behavior, not the doctrine bucket or who wrote it.
- [ ] **`logger.warning` / `logger.error` produces actionable signal, not decoration.** A warn followed by a silent default (`logger.warning(...); value = "other"`) is decoration; pick raise-or-cast and document the choice. Warn that's logged-and-ignored with no follow-up → `[BLOCK]`. Warn paired with a default-fallback → `APPROVE_WITH_NOTES` to pick one.
- [ ] **No duplicated rationale.** If the same comment ("proto3 omits zero values", "vendor returns null for blocked-state orders", etc.) applies to multiple fields, it appears ONCE — at the function header or above the relevant block — not on every field.
- [ ] Constants live at the narrowest scope that holds all callers: single-caller constants (e.g., a one-`format_*` `STATUS_MAP`) defined inside the function body; multi-function-same-resource constants above the first consumer; cross-resource constants at module top. Single-caller constant at module / class scope → `[BLOCK]`.
- [ ] No leading-underscore "private" names — methods (`_helper()`), attributes (`self._cache`), or locals (`_tmp`). Underscore privacy isn't enforced and isn't a convention this codebase uses. A class helper needing `self` → plain method (no underscore); not needing `self` → module-level function; used once → inline. **Advisory in new integrations; ignored in existing-integration edits** (dunders like `__init__` exempt).
- [ ] `page_token` default declared inline in the function signature, not via a `page_token=None` + `if page_token is None: page_token = ...` reset (unless the default genuinely depends on runtime state — exception documented in a comment)
- [ ] A function that splits the crawl range by date returns the finished window as `next_start` (tz-aware UTC, whole seconds, `start < next_start <= end`, `next_token=None`) instead of carrying the next window's start in the page token, and the resource stores crawl params. Lookbacks live in `crawl_start_overlap_minutes`, not in the function.
- [ ] No multi-line value expressions inside dict / kwargs literals — conditionals, `any()`/`all()` over iterables, multi-step transforms get hoisted to named variables above the constructor. Trivial inline ternaries and `.get(field, default)` are fine; the rule applies once the value wraps a line or scans an iterable. **Advisory (`APPROVE_WITH_NOTES`) on new builds.**
- [ ] **Every field in the API spec is represented.** A field present in the vendor's spec/OpenAPI but absent from `format_*` is a `[BLOCK]` finding, even if the sandbox sample didn't return it. Verify by reading the spec section in `api-summary.md` and diffing against the implementation.

For ID strategy:

- [ ] If `id` maps to a non-internal-ID source field (SKU, order_number, external reference), there's a documented uniqueness probe in `api-summary.md` "Field-mapping decisions" with date + operation + observed result. Missing probe → `[BLOCK]`.
- [ ] `ignore_link_tests()` is NOT overridden as a shortcut to silence failing link tests. Override only if the source data model genuinely doesn't link the resource pair, with an explanation in `api-summary.md`. Override-without-rationale → `[BLOCK]`.

For each test file touched:

- [ ] Only the `TestX` class — no module-level `def test_*` functions
- [ ] **No `pytest.fail("Not implemented")` stubs.** Either implement, or use `pytest.skip("...")` with a reason that points to the source-lacks-concept rationale.
- [ ] **No no-op `super()` overrides.** If the function just calls `super().<name>(...)`, delete the override.

For the auth bundle:

- [ ] The install path as a whole performs a live authenticated round-trip against the vendor. The check is satisfied if EITHER (a) `validate_integration_secret` makes an authenticated call and raises on failure, OR (b) `get_integration_secret` exchanges credentials for a token via an authenticated call (in which case `validate_integration_secret` may be `pass` with a comment noting auth happens in `get_integration_secret`).

For the request helper (`<name>_request`):

- [ ] 401 → `create_invalid_credentials_exception()`; 403 (or vendor-equivalent permission signal — text-matched `"AuthorizationException"`, JSON `"Access denied"`, HTTP 402-as-permission, etc.) → `create_permissions_exception(path)` recorded in `self.api_call_errors[path]` before raising. Collapsing the two into a single branch (`if response.status_code in [401, 403]:`) → `[BLOCK]`. The two exceptions are distinct surfaces — 401 reports connection-wide creds-broken, 403 reports per-URL scope-missing — and the framework's `ConnectionsError` row tracking depends on the split.
- [ ] `self.api_call_errors[path] = None` runs on the success path (after the `if not response.ok:` block, before `return response.json()`). Without it, prior connection errors for this URL **never clear** even after the customer fixes scope vendor-side. → `[BLOCK]` when the success-clear is missing on a helper that has any `create_permissions_exception` branch.
- [ ] Vendor returns permission info via text-match in a 2xx body or non-403 status and the helper raises `PokedexException` rather than recording the permission exception → `[BLOCK]`. Detect the signal; route through the same record-and-raise so the connection-error surface picks it up.

For the integration as a whole:

- [ ] **Diff is confined to the in-scope file set** (per "Files in scope — what an integration build may modify" above). Run `git diff --name-only main...HEAD` (or against the merge-base). Every modified path must match `pokedex/integrations/{type}/{name}/*`, `pokedex/test/integrations/{name}_test.py`, or fall under `.claude/`. Any other path — `pokedex/resources/<type>/*.py`, `pokedex/test/integrations/base_test.py`, `pokedex/utils/`, `pokedex/api.py`, `pokedex/config.py`, `pokedex/celery_tasks/`, `pokedex/lambda_handlers/`, framework base classes, other integrations — is an **automatic `[BLOCK]` with no `APPROVE_WITH_NOTES` path**. The fix is: revert the out-of-scope edit, escalate to the user with the workaround / separate-PR options, and resubmit. Reviewer must enumerate every offending file in the BLOCK finding.
- [ ] Targeted `yarn test` filtered to the integration + resource passes (or test failure is an actual bug, not a fixture mismatch from unrelated changes)
- [ ] `manifest.json` fields populated from the source context — not scaffolder placeholders. **`test/integrations/base_test.py::test_validate_manifest` is the canonical schema** — read it (around line 148) for the authoritative allow-list. Operational metadata (docs URL, UI URL, support contact, test account) lives on integration class methods (`Integration.docs()`, `ui()`, `support()`, `test_account()`), not the manifest.

For sandbox state — if the function-writer's `yarn crawl` returned data:

- [ ] Sample data **actually exercised the function path being claimed validated**. If all 100 orders are in `BLOCKED` status, `format_order`'s shipped-state path is unvalidated — flag as `APPROVE_WITH_NOTES` and surface the gap. If the function is for a resource with no sample data at all (e.g. zero orders returned), flag as `[BLOCK]` and recommend seeding before approval.

For shallow-fix detection — `[BLOCK]` if any of these appear without recorded rationale in `api-summary.md` "Field-mapping decisions":

- [ ] `IntegrationNotImplemented` assigned to any field
- [ ] `.get(field, default)` used (where default is anything other than what the comment justifies)
- [ ] A validator's `validate=` argument set to None, no-op, or weakened
- [ ] A new custom validator function added (not borrowed from canonical helpers)
- [ ] Schema field's `required=False` changed to bypass validation
- [ ] A test assertion modified to expect a different value than before (vs. adding new assertions)
- [ ] A field synthesized from `now()`, hardcoded values, or computed defaults instead of pulled from source
- [ ] An `except` block that swallows exceptions silently (catches and continues without re-raising or logging)

### Evidence-required exchange (shallow-fix family triggers a conversation, not a one-pass critique)

The shallow-fix family — validator disables, `Strict()→Loose()`, custom-validator-that-bypasses, schema field weakened from required to optional, `IntegrationNotImplemented` on a field the spec says exists, `.get()` with a default where the spec doesn't say "sometimes absent" — represents **judgment failures masquerading as mechanical ones**. One-pass critique ("add rationale") doesn't surface the deeper question: *did the writer miss a better field?*

A reviewer that flags `validate_financial_info_with_line_items` disabled should not just demand rationale — they should pull the writer's reasoning into the open. Maybe the writer compared `total` against `sum(line_items)` and they don't match, when they should have checked `subtotal` or summed `unit_price * quantity` instead of using `total_price`. The conversation surfaces that.

**How it works:**

When the reviewer detects a shallow-fix-family finding, the report's `[BLOCK]` entry includes an **Evidence required** subsection demanding four pieces from the writer:

1. **Raw response sample** — paste the actual JSON/XML body of one record showing the issue (not a summary, not "the field was null"; the actual body).
2. **Alternative source fields considered** — *named*, with their values from the raw response. Not "I considered alternatives"; specifically `"checked total=$45.20, subtotal=$42.00, total_price=$50.50, grand_total=null"`.
3. **For each alternative, why it doesn't work** — or pick one and re-implement.
4. **Justification for the chosen path** — valid only after exhausting the alternatives above.

The writer's next-iteration response (via `SendMessage` to the persisted writer) must include a structured **Evidence responses** section mirroring the request, one block per evidence-required finding.

The fresh reviewer on the next iteration reads the prior reviewer's report + the writer's evidence response as context, and evaluates whether the evidence justifies the chosen path. They can:

- `APPROVE` — evidence is concrete and the chosen path is defensible.
- `BLOCK` with a refined request — "you considered fields A, B, C but missed field D — check that against the raw response."
- `BLOCK` on insufficient evidence — "raw response wasn't pasted" / "alternatives weren't named" / "rationale was hand-wavy."

The 3-attempt iteration cap (see `references/loop-control.md`) encompasses evidence-required exchanges. If still unresolved at attempt 3, escalate to user with the full dialogue.

**Mechanical-fix findings don't trigger this.** Docstring present, comprehension used, `or None` suffix — these are clear violations with clear fixes. One-pass critique stays.

#### Reviewer report format addition

When a finding qualifies, the report's `Mandatory rule violations` block includes:

```markdown
- [BLOCK] {file}:{line} — {validator/schema/IntegrationNotImplemented rule}. {one-line description}

  **Evidence required (respond in your Evidence responses section):**
  1. Raw response sample for {record type} — paste full JSON/XML body of one sample.
  2. Alternative source fields considered for {target field}: list named, with values.
  3. Per alternative: why it doesn't work, or pick it and re-implement.
  4. Justification for the chosen path (only valid after exhausting alternatives).
```

#### Writer response format addition

When responding to evidence-required findings, the writer adds an **Evidence responses** section to the return notes (or to the SendMessage response):

```markdown
## Evidence responses

### {file}:{line} — {finding}

**Raw sample:** {paste full record body}

**Alternatives considered:**
- `field_a`: <value> — <why considered, why rejected, or "implemented using this">
- `field_b`: <value> — <reason>
- `field_c`: <value> — <reason>

**Chosen path:** {what was implemented, or "stands as disabled because [reason that survives the alternatives above]"}
```

The writer must do this work honestly — *if* during evidence gathering you realize the reviewer is right and there's a better field, re-implement using that field. Don't defend the original disable just because it was the initial choice.

